# Echo Script v1 — Before Leaving Shore
@label start
@background empty_station
@narrate Seven minutes remain before the last coastal train. The tide reaches the platform before the announcement does.
@speaker Me
@say Have you really decided to leave?
@background yao_at_station
@speaker Yao
@say A decision does not happen in a single moment. It is more like the tide—by the time you notice, the sand beneath you has changed.
@speaker Me
@say If we eventually forget those who leave, did meeting them mean anything?
@speaker Yao
@say You are treating meaning as something that must be preserved forever.
@say When a song ends, the silence does not prove it was never heard.
@choice Memory gives farewell its meaning => memory | Forgetting is why farewell matters => forgetting | Some words exist without being spoken => unsaid
@label memory
@set answer = memory
@speaker Me
@say I still want to remember. As long as I remember, nothing is completely lost.
@speaker Yao
@say Then remember change, not a version of me pinned to today. A memory that forbids change becomes another kind of forgetting.
@jump ending
@label forgetting
@set answer = forgetting
@speaker Me
@say Perhaps we say goodbye carefully because we know we will forget.
@speaker Yao
@say Yes. Farewell does not prevent forgetting. It admits that this moment truly belonged to us.
@jump ending
@label unsaid
@set answer = unsaid
@speaker Me
@say I prepared so many words. Now it seems all right not to say them.
@speaker Yao
@say Silence is not empty. It simply leaves the explanation to our future selves.
@jump ending
@label ending
@background empty_station
@narrate A distant light moves along the coast. She walks toward the door, neither looking back nor deliberately refusing to.
@if answer == memory -> memory_end
@if answer == forgetting -> forgetting_end
@jump unsaid_end
@label memory_end
@end Reverberation | What I remembered was not her departing silhouette, but how the sea became a slightly different color every year afterward.
@label forgetting_end
@end Tide | Years later I forgot the exact words, yet whenever train doors close, I still nod gently toward the empty air.
@label unsaid_end
@end Negative Space | We never completed that farewell. So it never ended; it only learned to exist without asking for an answer.
